@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

Code Review shows a pull request in a @file{*Code Review*} buffer: its
header (author, title, state, branches, labels, assignees, reviewers),
its commits, its description, the conversation and the diff of every
changed file, with the review comments placed on the lines they were
made on.  Outdated comments are shown with the diff hunk they were made
on.

From that buffer you can:

@itemize
@item
read and reply to comments, and add comments to any line of the diff,
including code suggestions;
@item
approve, request changes or comment, with an overall feedback message;
@item
set labels, assignees, milestones, reviewers, the title and the
description;
@item
react to comments with emoji, and promote a comment to a new issue;
@item
review a single commit, save an unfinished review and resume it later;
@item
visit a changed file in your local clone, or a binary file in Dired.
@end itemize

Not every feature is available on every forge; @pxref{Forge Support}.

@section About this fork

This is a fork of
@uref{https://github.com/wandersoncferreira/code-review}, which is
unmaintained since December 2022 and no longer loads with current
versions of its dependencies.  The fork's @code{port} branch:

@itemize
@item
ports the database layer to current EmacSQL and closql, and the Gitlab
requests to current Ghub, which dropped @code{glab-get} and similar
functions;
@item
removes Bitbucket support;
@item
fixes Gitlab replies, approval and review feedback against current
Gitlab, and reviews each merge request on its own Gitlab instance.
@end itemize

Only Gitlab has been tested since the port, on gitlab.com and on a
self-hosted instance.  The Github code compiles but is untested.

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

Do not install @code{code-review} from MELPA: that is the unported
upstream version.  Install the fork's @code{port} branch with
@code{package-vc}, which also installs the dependencies from the package
archives.  MELPA must be in @code{package-archives}.

@lisp
(package-vc-install
 '(code-review :url "https://github.com/ilitzroth/code-review"
               :branch "port"))
@end lisp

Or, from a local clone:

@lisp
(package-vc-install-from-checkout "~/path/to/code-review" "code-review")
@end lisp

@cindex manual, installing
This manual comes with the package as @file{code-review.info}, with a
@file{dir} file next to it, at the top of the repository.  When the
package is installed with @code{package-vc-install}, the package
directory is the clone, so Emacs adds it to @code{Info-directory-list}
and the manual appears in @kbd{C-h i}.  A package installed with
@code{package-vc-install-from-checkout} lives in a separate directory
that only loads the clone's autoloads, so add the clone yourself:

@lisp
(with-eval-after-load 'info
  (add-to-list 'Info-directory-list "~/path/to/code-review"))
@end lisp

Then configure access to your forge: @pxref{Gitlab}, or @pxref{Github}.

@node Getting Started
@chapter Getting Started

@findex code-review-start
@findex code-review-forge-pr-at-point
There are two ways to open a review:

@table @kbd
@item M-x code-review-start
Prompt for the URL of a pull request or merge request and open it.

@item M-x code-review-forge-pr-at-point
Open the pull request at point in a Forge or Magit buffer, or the one
shown in a Forge topic buffer.  @xref{Key Binding Suggestions}, for a
binding.
@end table

The basic workflow:

@enumerate
@item
Move to a line of the diff and press @kbd{RET} to write a comment on it.
@xref{Comments and Replies}.
@item
Press @kbd{RET} on a comment that is already on the forge to reply to it,
or on one of your local comments to edit it.
@item
Press @kbd{r s f} to write your overall feedback.
@item
Press @kbd{r a} to approve, @kbd{r r} to request changes (Github only) or
@kbd{r c} to send your comments without a verdict.
@xref{Submitting a Review}.
@end enumerate

Your comments stay local until you submit the review, so you can write
them in any order and change them first.

@node The Review Buffer
@chapter The Review Buffer

@cindex review buffer
The review buffer is a Magit section buffer: @kbd{TAB} folds and unfolds
a section, @kbd{n} and @kbd{p} move between sections and @kbd{M-n} and
@kbd{M-p} between sibling sections, as in Magit.  It has these sections,
from top to bottom:

@itemize
@item
the header: author, title, state, branches, milestone, labels, projects,
draft state, assignees, suggested reviewers and reviewers;
@item
the commits, with their CI state on Github;
@item
the description;
@item
@samp{Your Review Feedback}, the overall message of your review;
@item
the conversation: comments that are not on a line of the diff;
@item
the files changed, each with its diff and the comments on its lines.
@end itemize

Many header lines are commands: press @kbd{RET} on the title, the labels,
the milestone or the assignee to change them (@pxref{Editing the Pull
Request}), on an author to visit their profile, and on a suggested
reviewer to request their review.

@kindex r
@kindex RET
@kindex G
@kindex C-c C-s
@kindex C-c RET
@kindex C-RET
@findex code-review-transient-api
@findex code-review-comment-add-or-edit
@findex code-review-reload
@findex code-review-comment-code-suggestion
@findex code-review-submit-single-diff-comment-at-point
@findex code-review-visit-file-at-point
@table @kbd
@item r
Show the Transient menu with all review commands
(@code{code-review-transient-api}).

@item RET
On a diff line, write a comment on it; on a comment, reply to it; on one
of your local comments, edit it (@code{code-review-comment-add-or-edit}).

@item C-c C-s
Write a code suggestion for the diff line at point
(@code{code-review-comment-code-suggestion}).  Github only.

@item C-c RET
Write a comment on the diff line at point and send it right away,
outside a review (@code{code-review-submit-single-diff-comment-at-point}).

@item C-c C-k
Delete the local comment or the feedback at point.

@item C-c C-r
Add a reaction to the comment or description at point
(@pxref{Reactions}).

@item C-c C-n
Promote the comment at point to a new issue.

@item C-RET
Visit the file at point in your local clone (@pxref{Visiting Files}).

@item G
Reload the pull request from the forge (@code{code-review-reload}).
@end table

@findex code-review-toggle-display-all-comments
@findex code-review-toggle-display-top-level-comments
@findex code-review-toggle-display-diff-comments
The commands @code{code-review-toggle-display-all-comments},
@code{code-review-toggle-display-top-level-comments} and
@code{code-review-toggle-display-diff-comments} hide or show comments,
which helps when reading the diff of a busy pull request.

@findex code-review-comment-jump-next
@findex code-review-comment-jump-previous
@code{code-review-comment-jump-next} and
@code{code-review-comment-jump-previous} move to the next and previous
comment; they have no default binding (@pxref{Key Binding
Suggestions}).

@cindex commits, reviewing one
@findex code-review-commit-at-point
@kindex b
Press @kbd{RET} on a commit to review that commit alone, in a separate
@file{*Code Review Commit*} buffer (@code{code-review-commit-at-point}).
Press @kbd{b} there to go back to the whole pull request.

@node Comments and Replies
@chapter Comments and Replies

@cindex comment buffer
@kindex C-c C-c
@kindex C-c C-k
@kindex C-c @@
@findex code-review-comment-commit
@findex code-review-comment-quit
@findex code-review-input-mention-user-at-point
Writing a comment, a reply or the feedback opens a
@file{*Code Review Comment*} buffer in Markdown mode:

@table @kbd
@item C-c C-c
Save the comment locally (@code{code-review-comment-commit}).  Comments
written with @kbd{C-c RET} and @kbd{r s c} are sent right away instead.

@item C-c C-k
Cancel (@code{code-review-comment-quit}).

@item C-c @@
Insert a mention of a user, chosen with completion
(@code{code-review-input-mention-user-at-point}).
@end table

@cindex local comments
Saved comments are shown in the review buffer as local comments.  They
are sent with the review (@pxref{Submitting a Review}).  Press @kbd{RET}
on a local comment to edit it, and @kbd{C-c C-k} to delete it.

@cindex code suggestions
@kbd{C-c C-s} on a diff line opens the comment buffer with that line in a
@code{suggestion} block, which you change into the code you suggest.
Github shows it with a button to apply the suggestion.  This is not
supported on Gitlab yet.

@node Submitting a Review
@chapter Submitting a Review

@cindex submitting a review
Press @kbd{r} in the review buffer for the Transient menu.  Its
@samp{Review} group sends your local comments and replies, the feedback
and a verdict:

@kindex r a
@kindex r r
@kindex r c
@findex code-review-submit-approve
@findex code-review-submit-request-changes
@findex code-review-submit-comments
@table @kbd
@item r a
Approve (@code{code-review-submit-approve}).

@item r r
Request changes (@code{code-review-submit-request-changes}).  Github
only.

@item r c
Send the comments without a verdict (@code{code-review-submit-comments}).
@end table

@cindex feedback
@kindex r s f
@findex code-review-set-feedback
@findex code-review-delete-feedback
The feedback is the overall message of the review.  Set it with
@kbd{r s f} (@code{code-review-set-feedback}) or @kbd{RET} on
@samp{Your Review Feedback}, and delete it with @kbd{C-c C-k} there
(@code{code-review-delete-feedback}).  Github requires a feedback message
to request changes.

The @samp{Fast track} group sends things without a review:

@kindex r l
@kindex r p
@kindex r s c
@kindex r s C
@findex code-review-submit-lgtm
@findex code-review-submit-only-replies
@findex code-review-submit-single-top-level-comment
@vindex code-review-lgtm-message
@table @kbd
@item r l
Approve with the message in @code{code-review-lgtm-message}
(@code{code-review-submit-lgtm}).

@item r p
Send only your replies to existing comments
(@code{code-review-submit-only-replies}).

@item r s c
Write a comment on the conversation and send it right away
(@code{code-review-submit-single-top-level-comment}).

@item r s C
Write a comment on the diff line at point and send it right away
(@code{code-review-submit-single-diff-comment-at-point}).
@end table

@vindex code-review-always-restrict-approval?
Set @code{code-review-always-restrict-approval?} to non-@code{nil} to
refuse approving a pull request whose CI state is not successful
(Github).

@node Editing the Pull Request
@chapter Editing the Pull Request

The @samp{Setters} group of the Transient menu changes the pull request
itself.  These changes are sent right away.

@kindex r s r
@kindex r s y
@kindex r s a
@kindex r s m
@kindex r s l
@kindex r s t
@kindex r s d
@findex code-review-request-reviews
@findex code-review-set-yourself-assignee
@findex code-review-set-assignee
@findex code-review-set-milestone
@findex code-review-set-label
@findex code-review-set-title
@findex code-review-set-description
@table @kbd
@item r s r
Request reviews (@code{code-review-request-reviews}).

@item r s y
Assign yourself (@code{code-review-set-yourself-assignee}).

@item r s a
Set the assignee (@code{code-review-set-assignee}).

@item r s m
Set the milestone (@code{code-review-set-milestone}).  Github only sets
it for users with push access, and silently ignores it otherwise.

@item r s l
Set the labels (@code{code-review-set-label}).

@item r s t
Edit the title (@code{code-review-set-title}).

@item r s d
Edit the description (@code{code-review-set-description}).
@end table

@cindex multi-value selection
Labels, reviewers and other fields that take several values are read
with @code{completing-read-multiple}: separate the values with commas,
and press @kbd{TAB} to see the candidates.  An empty input removes all
labels.

@node Reactions
@chapter Reactions

@cindex reactions
On Github you can react with emoji to the description and to any comment.

@itemize
@item
Press @kbd{C-c C-r} on a comment or the description to add a reaction.
@item
Press @kbd{RET} on an emoji to add your reaction or remove it.
@end itemize

@findex code-review-description-reaction-at-point
@findex code-review-code-comment-reaction-at-point
@findex code-review-conversation-reaction-at-point
@vindex code-review-description-section-map
@vindex code-review-code-comment-section-map
@vindex code-review-comment-section-map
Each kind of comment has its own command, bound in its own keymap.  To
use another key, rebind them:

@lisp
(define-key code-review-description-section-map (kbd "C-c C-r")
  #'code-review-description-reaction-at-point)
(define-key code-review-code-comment-section-map (kbd "C-c C-r")
  #'code-review-code-comment-reaction-at-point)
(define-key code-review-comment-section-map (kbd "C-c C-r")
  #'code-review-conversation-reaction-at-point)
@end lisp

@node Merging
@chapter Merging

@cindex merging
@kindex r m m
@kindex r m r
@kindex r m s
@findex code-review-merge-merge
@findex code-review-merge-rebase
@findex code-review-merge-squash
The @samp{Merge} group of the Transient menu merges the pull request on
Github: @kbd{r m m} with a merge commit (@code{code-review-merge-merge}),
@kbd{r m r} by rebasing (@code{code-review-merge-rebase}) and @kbd{r m s}
by squashing (@code{code-review-merge-squash}).

This is experimental.  The commit title and message are taken from the
pull request's title and description, and cannot be changed.

@node Saving and Resuming Reviews
@chapter Saving and Resuming Reviews

@cindex unfinished reviews
@kindex r C-c C-s
@kindex r C-c C-r
@findex code-review-save-unfinished-review
@findex code-review-open-unfinished-review
@findex code-review-recover-unfinished-review
Local comments live in a database, so a review can be left unfinished:

@table @kbd
@item r C-c C-s
Save the review (@code{code-review-save-unfinished-review}).

@item r C-c C-r
Choose a saved review and open it again
(@code{code-review-open-unfinished-review}).

@item M-x code-review-recover-unfinished-review
Open the saved review of the pull request at a URL.
@end table

@vindex code-review-db-database-file
The database is @code{code-review-db-database-file}, by default
@file{code-review-db-file.sqlite} in @code{user-emacs-directory}.

@node Visiting Files
@chapter Visiting Files

@cindex visiting files
@kindex C-RET
@findex code-review-visit-file-at-point
@kbd{C-RET} (@code{code-review-visit-file-at-point}) on a diff line or a
file heading opens the file in your local clone of the repository, the
worktree Forge knows for it, at the line at point.  When the clone is not
at the pull request's head commit, it offers to check out the pull
request's branch there, but only if the clone has no uncommitted changes.
Then the code you read, and tools like Eglot, see the pull request's
version.

@cindex binary files
@vindex code-review-download-dir
@kindex C-c C-v
@kbd{RET} on a binary file, such as an image, downloads it to
@code{code-review-download-dir} and opens that directory in Dired.
@kbd{C-c C-v} opens the file on the forge instead.

@node Forge Support
@chapter Forge Support

@cindex forge support
Features supported by each forge:

@multitable {Save and resume an unfinished review} {experimental} {Gitlab}
@headitem Feature @tab Github @tab Gitlab
@item Add comments @tab yes @tab yes
@item Reply to comments @tab yes @tab yes
@item Send only replies @tab yes @tab yes
@item Send a review with comments @tab yes @tab yes
@item Approve @tab yes @tab yes
@item Request changes @tab yes @tab no
@item Send a single comment @tab yes @tab yes
@item Send a single diff comment @tab yes @tab yes
@item Code suggestions @tab yes @tab no
@item Set labels @tab yes @tab yes
@item Set the assignee @tab yes @tab no
@item Set the milestone @tab yes @tab no
@item Edit the title @tab yes @tab yes
@item Edit the description @tab yes @tab no
@item Request reviewers @tab yes @tab no
@item Merge @tab experimental @tab no
@item Reactions @tab yes @tab no
@item Promote a comment to an issue @tab yes @tab no
@item Save and resume an unfinished review @tab yes @tab yes
@item Get and visit binary files @tab yes @tab yes
@item CI state of commits @tab yes @tab no
