@node Gitlab Authentication
@section Authentication

@cindex Gitlab, token
@vindex code-review-auth-login-marker
If you use Forge, reuse its token and skip the rest of this section:

@lisp
(setq code-review-auth-login-marker 'forge)
@end lisp

Otherwise, create a personal access token on Gitlab with the @code{api}
scope, and add a line with your login and the token to your auth source
file, usually @file{~/.authinfo.gpg}:

@example
machine gitlab.com/api login yourlogin^code-review password TOKEN
@end example

The token is also found when the line names the REST endpoint instead,
@samp{gitlab.com/api/v4}, which is where Forge stores its tokens.

@node Gitlab Instances
@section Gitlab Instances

@cindex Gitlab, self-hosted
@cindex self-hosted Gitlab
Each review uses the Gitlab instance of its merge request, so reviews on
gitlab.com and on self-hosted instances work side by side without any
configuration:

@itemize
@item
From Forge (@code{code-review-forge-pr-at-point}), the API host is the
one Forge uses for the repository.
@item
From a URL (@code{code-review-start}), any
@samp{https://@var{host}/@dots{}/-/merge_requests/@var{n}} URL is
recognized.  The API host is the one in @var{host}'s entry in
@code{forge-alist}, or else @samp{@var{host}/api}.
@end itemize

@cindex Gitlab, username
Ghub finds your username in the Git variable
@code{gitlab.@var{host}.user}, or else @code{gitlab.user}, as for Forge:

@example
git config --global gitlab.git.example.com.user yourlogin
@end example

@vindex code-review-gitlab-host
@vindex code-review-gitlab-graphql-host
@vindex code-review-gitlab-base-url
The options @code{code-review-gitlab-host} and
@code{code-review-gitlab-graphql-host} are only used for reviews saved
before the host was recorded, and for an instance whose API host is not
@samp{@var{host}/api} and not in @code{forge-alist}.  In that case set
them, with @code{code-review-gitlab-base-url}, for that instance:

@lisp
(setq code-review-gitlab-base-url "git.example.com"       ; MR URLs
      code-review-gitlab-host "git.example.com/api"       ; REST
      code-review-gitlab-graphql-host "git.example.com/api"); GraphQL
@end lisp

@cindex insecure hosts
@vindex ghub-insecure-hosts
For an instance served over HTTP rather than HTTPS, add it to
@code{ghub-insecure-hosts}:

@lisp
(setq ghub-insecure-hosts '("git.example.com"))
@end lisp

@node Approving on Gitlab
@section Approving on Gitlab

@kbd{r a} approves the merge request.  Gitlab's approve API takes no
message, so the feedback set with @kbd{r s f} is posted as a separate
comment next to the approval.

A Gitlab project can require users to enter their password to approve a
merge request; then you are asked for your username and password.

Requesting changes (@kbd{r r}) is not supported on Gitlab.

@node Github
@chapter Github

The Github code has not been tested since the port.

@menu
* Github Authentication::
* Github Enterprise::
@end menu

@node Github Authentication
@section Authentication

@cindex Github, token
Code Review needs a Github token to fetch pull requests and submit
reviews for you.  With @code{code-review-auth-login-marker} set to
@code{forge}, it uses Forge's token.  Otherwise:

@enumerate
@item
Create a personal access token on Github with the @code{repo} scope.  On
Github Enterprise, also give it the @code{read:discussion} and
@code{write:discussion} scopes.
@item
Add a line with your login and the token to your auth source file,
usually @file{~/.authinfo.gpg}:

@example
machine api.github.com login yourlogin^code-review password TOKEN
@end example
@end enumerate

@node Github Enterprise
@section Github Enterprise

@cindex Github Enterprise
On Github Enterprise, the token must be in your auth source under each
host and path Code Review uses.  For example, for
@samp{ghe.example.com}:

@example
machine ghe.example.com             login yourlogin^code-review password TOKEN
machine ghe.example.com/api         login yourlogin^code-review password TOKEN
machine ghe.example.com/api/v3      login yourlogin^code-review password TOKEN
machine ghe.example.com/api/graphql login yourlogin^code-review password TOKEN
@end example

@vindex code-review-github-host
@vindex code-review-github-graphql-host
@vindex code-review-github-base-url
Then set the hosts.  @code{/graphql} is appended to
@code{code-review-github-graphql-host} automatically.
@code{code-review-github-base-url} is only needed to start reviews from a
URL with @code{code-review-start}.

@lisp
(setq code-review-github-host "ghe.example.com/api/v3"
      code-review-github-graphql-host "ghe.example.com/api"
      code-review-github-base-url "ghe.example.com")
@end lisp

Code Review also needs to know your username on the instance:

@example
git config --global github.ghe.example.com.user yourlogin
git config --global github.ghe.example.com/api.user yourlogin
git config --global github.ghe.example.com/api/v3.user yourlogin
git config --global github.ghe.example.com/api/graphql.user yourlogin
@end example

To also review pull requests on github.com, keep those projects under one
directory and reset the hosts there in @file{.dir-locals.el}:

@lisp
((nil . ((code-review-github-base-url . "github.com")
         (code-review-github-graphql-host . "api.github.com")
         (code-review-github-host . "api.github.com"))))
@end lisp

